---
import ArticleLayout from '../../components/ArticleLayout.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is a rental make-ready?',
    a: 'A make-ready is the work that takes a rental from the day a tenant moves out to the day it is ready to show and lease: cleaning, paint, repairs, flooring, and a final inspection. It is also called a turnover or a turn.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What order should you do a make-ready in?',
    a: 'Work top to bottom and dirty to clean: repairs first, then paint, then flooring, then the final deep clean, so later steps do not undo earlier ones. Booking trades in the wrong order is the most common cause of a turn dragging on.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How fast can a rental be made ready?',
    a: 'A coordinated single-family make-ready typically takes 4 to 7 days when one crew handles every trade in sequence. It stretches to weeks when a landlord schedules each contractor separately.',
  },
];
---
<ArticleLayout
  slug="rental-make-ready-checklist"
  eyebrow="Landlord Checklist"
  title="The Rental Make-Ready Checklist Landlords Actually Use"
  description="A room-by-room make-ready checklist to take a rental from move-out to rent-ready fast, in the order that keeps vacancy days low."
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p>
    A rental make-ready is just a sequence, and the sequence is the whole
    game. Do the steps in the right order, repairs before paint, paint before
    flooring, cleaning last, and the whole turn moves in <strong>4 to 7
    days</strong>. Do them out of order and you repaint a wall you just
    dinged, or clean a floor before the installers track through it. Here is
    the checklist, in the order that keeps vacancy days low.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 1 — Walk-through and scope (Day 1)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Photograph every room for the move-out condition record</li>
    <li>Note damage vs. wear and tear for the deposit (<a href="/articles/wear-and-tear-vs-damage-tennessee/">how to tell them apart in Tennessee</a>)</li>
    <li>Build the punch list: everything broken, missing, or worn</li>
    <li>Decide the tier: refresh, standard turn, or full make-ready</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Step 2 — Repairs and punch list (Days 1–2)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Drywall patches, nail holes, and any wall damage</li>
    <li>Doors, locks, handles, and cabinet hardware</li>
    <li>Plumbing: faucets, toilets, shut-off valves, supply lines</li>
    <li>Electrical: outlets, switches, light fixtures, smoke and CO detectors</li>
    <li>HVAC filter swap and a quick system check</li>
    <li>Caulk and grout in kitchens and baths</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Repairs come first because everything after this covers them up.</p>

  <h2>Step 3 — Paint (Days 2–3)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Touch up scuffs and marks, or full repaint if needed</li>
    <li>Prime any stains or patched drywall first</li>
    <li>Use one standard portfolio color so future turns are touch-ups, not repaints</li>
    <li>Paint before flooring so drips do not land on new floors</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Step 4 — Flooring (Day 3–4)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Clean carpet, or replace if stains or matting will not come out</li>
    <li>Install LVP if upgrading, it outlasts carpet across several tenants</li>
    <li>Replace worn transition strips and baseboards</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Step 5 — Deep clean (Day 4–5)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Full move-out clean: kitchens, baths, floors, baseboards, fixtures</li>
    <li>Appliance deep clean inside and out</li>
    <li>Windows, blinds, and light bulbs (replace any dead ones)</li>
    <li>Cleaning goes last so no trade tracks through a clean unit</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Step 6 — Curb appeal and final inspection (Day 5–7)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Mow, edge, trim, and clear beds; pressure wash if needed</li>
    <li>Final walk-through against this checklist</li>
    <li>Move-in condition photos for the next tenant's baseline</li>
    <li>Confirm it is listing-ready and shoot listing photos</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="takeaway">
    <p>
      The reason turns drag is not the work, it is the gaps between
      contractors. One crew running this sequence back to back is what turns a
      three-week vacancy into a one-week one, and on an $1,800 rental that is
      roughly $840 in saved rent. See
      <a href="/articles/rental-turnover-cost-nashville/">what a turnover
      actually costs in Nashville</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Work the list in order, keep one crew moving through it, and a make-ready
    stays fast and predictable. Upkeep runs
    <a href="/turnovers/">packaged make-readies</a> across Middle Tennessee on
    exactly this sequence, with a flat quote before the unit is vacant. One
    <a href="/work-order/">service request</a> gets it scheduled.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
